1
00:00:01,070 --> 00:00:22,100
The lab environment is hazardous by nature. The actual risk is largely

2
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:27,080
determined by you and those working with you. It's your responsibility to know and

3
00:00:27,080 --> 00:00:31,960
follow the rules and be able to recognize potential safety hazards.

4
00:00:31,960 --> 00:00:36,600
The first consideration is proper dress. What you wear in the lab can help

5
00:00:36,600 --> 00:00:41,280
prevent serious, even fatal injuries. Often you'll be working with chemicals

6
00:00:41,280 --> 00:00:45,680
that could harm you if they come into contact with your skin. So the best way

7
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:49,640
to prevent skin exposure is to wear clothes that cover your entire body.

8
00:00:49,640 --> 00:00:54,560
Shorts and sandals leave your skin exposed, so instead wear long pants and

9
00:00:54,560 --> 00:00:59,480
closed-toed shoes. Remove jewelry before entering the lab and tie back long hair.

10
00:00:59,480 --> 00:01:04,880
Bring only the things that you need into the lab. Leave all personal items like

11
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:10,000
backpacks, purses, or jackets outside so they don't become contaminated. Now that

12
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,720
you're properly dressed for the lab, let's take a look at personal protective

13
00:01:13,720 --> 00:01:21,160
equipment or PPE. For general lab work, a lab coat, safety glasses or goggles, and

14
00:01:21,160 --> 00:01:25,720
gloves are required. Always button your coat and try to keep the cuffs tucked

15
00:01:25,720 --> 00:01:30,280
into your gloves. Wear a chemical apron if you're working with splash hazards,

16
00:01:30,280 --> 00:01:34,720
volatile or reactive solutions. Safety glasses can protect your eyes from

17
00:01:34,720 --> 00:01:39,600
flying debris. However, these glasses won't protect you from splash hazards.

18
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:45,240
That's why you may need to wear chemical resistant safety goggles. Always

19
00:01:45,240 --> 00:01:50,000
wear gloves in the lab. Be sure to choose the most appropriate type. You need

20
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:53,480
gloves that are resistant to the chemicals you'll be handling. These

21
00:01:53,480 --> 00:01:58,600
gloves are non-absorbent. Even so, chemicals can leach through over time,

22
00:01:58,600 --> 00:02:02,800
so it's a good idea to change your gloves and wash your hands frequently,

23
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:08,000
especially if they've come into contact with chemicals. Thermal and puncture

24
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:12,720
resistant gloves are used for handling extremely hot or cold materials, such as

25
00:02:12,720 --> 00:02:17,960
when using the autoclave, handling dry ice, or handling sharps. Some chemicals

26
00:02:17,960 --> 00:02:23,400
produce dangerous vapors. A respirator can protect you. However, by law, you must

27
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:28,000
first complete the proper training. So ask your lab manager or instructor about

28
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:33,840
respirator training. Always remove PPE and wash your hands before leaving the

29
00:02:33,840 --> 00:02:38,560
lab and entering public areas. And be aware that you can spread chemical or

30
00:02:38,560 --> 00:02:43,640
biological contamination by touching items such as light switches, doorknobs, or

31
00:02:43,640 --> 00:02:48,480
even phones while your gloves are still on. Dressing for the lab and wearing

32
00:02:48,480 --> 00:02:52,560
personal protective equipment will minimize the risk for exposure or harm,

33
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:57,960
but clothing and PPE aren't enough to keep you safe. We'll take a closer look

34
00:02:57,960 --> 00:03:02,000
at safety equipment in our next lesson.
